{0 Workflows}

A {{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/workflows/}Workflow} is a durable,
multi-step program. Each step runs at least once, its result is stored, and the
runtime can sleep, retry and resume the program for days. {!Workero.Workflow}
covers both halves: the class that runs the steps, and the binding that starts
and controls instances.

{1 Write a Workflow}

Register the class under a name at module initialisation. The function gets the
[env], the event and the step object:

{@ocaml[
open Workero
module Step = Workflow.Step

let () =
  Workflow.register "Greet" (fun env event step ->
      let name =
        Step.do_ step "read name" (fun _ ->
            match event.Workflow.payload with
            | `Assoc [ ("name", `String n) ] -> `String n
            | _ -> `String "world")
      in
      Step.sleep step "pause" (Workflow.Duration.seconds 10);
      let greeting =
        Step.do_ step "greet"
          ~retries:
            (Step.retries ~limit:3 ~delay:(Workflow.Duration.seconds 5)
               ~backoff:Step.Exponential ())
          ~timeout:(Workflow.Duration.minutes 1)
          (fun { Step.attempt } ->
            let res = fetch "https://example.com/greeting" in
            Console.log (Printf.sprintf "greeting, attempt %d" attempt);
            `String (Response.body_text res))
      in
      `Assoc [ ("name", name); ("greeting", greeting) ])
]}

The function runs in a fiber and may call {!Workero.Promise.await}. Its result
is the output of the instance. An exception that it raises fails the instance.

[workero build] runs the program once in [workerd] and exports every registered
class from [index.js], like Durable Object and entrypoint classes. It writes
[workflow.js] only when a Workflow is registered, so a Worker without Workflows
imports nothing new. Declare the class in [wrangler.toml]; [class_name] is the
name given to {!Workero.Workflow.register}:

{@toml[
[[workflows]]
name = "greet"
binding = "GREET"
class_name = "Greet"
]}

{1 Replay}

The runtime runs the function again after every sleep, wait, retry and
eviction. {!Workero.Workflow.Step.do_} returns the stored result of a step that
already finished and does not call its callback. So:

- Code outside the steps runs again each time. It must be deterministic: do not
  read the clock or random numbers there. Produce the value inside a step and
  return it, as in the example.
- OCaml values do not live between runs. Keep what later steps need in the
  results of earlier steps.
- A step is identified by its name and the number of calls with that name. Do
  not rename or reorder the steps of instances that already run.
- A step callback runs at least once. Make it idempotent.

{1 Steps}

- {!Workero.Workflow.Step.do_}: run a callback, store its JSON result. The
  callback gets a {!Workero.Workflow.Step.context} with the [attempt] number: 1
  on the first try, 2 on the first retry. [retries]
  sets [limit], [delay] and [backoff] ([Constant], [Linear] or [Exponential]);
  [timeout] applies to each attempt. Without them the runtime uses 5 retries, a
  10 second delay, exponential backoff and a 10 minute timeout.
- {!Workero.Workflow.Step.sleep} and {!Workero.Workflow.Step.sleep_until}: wait
  for a {!Workero.Workflow.Duration.t} or until a time in milliseconds since the
  epoch, like {!Workero.Date.now}.
- {!Workero.Workflow.Step.wait_for_event}: wait for an event of a given type,
  sent with {!Workero.Workflow.Instance.send_event}, and return its payload.
  It fails with {!Workero.Js_error} after the timeout (24 hours by default).
  Catch the error to go on.

A callback that raises fails the attempt, and the runtime retries it. Raise
{!Workero.Workflow.Non_retryable} to fail without retries; the runtime sees a
[NonRetryableError]. After the last attempt, {!Workero.Workflow.Step.do_} raises
{!Workero.Js_error} in the function; catch it to keep the instance running.

Step results, event payloads and params are JSON values ({!Workero.JSON}). The
JSON parser reads integers as OCaml [int], which has 31 bits in a Worker: a step
that returns a number above 1073741823, such as [Date.now ()], fails with a
parse error. Return such a value as a string.

{1 Start and control instances}

Read the binding with {!Workero.Workflow.of_env}:

{@ocaml[
let handler req env _ctx =
  let greet = Workflow.of_env env "GREET" in
  let instance =
    Workflow.create greet ~params:(`Assoc [ ("name", `String "Ana") ])
  in
  let state = Workflow.Instance.status instance in
  Response.text
    (Workflow.Instance.id instance
    ^ (match state.status with
      | Workflow.Instance.Complete -> " complete"
      | _ -> " running"))
]}

{!Workero.Workflow.create} takes an optional [id] and [params]; the params are
the [payload] of the event. {!Workero.Workflow.create_batch} starts up to 100
instances and returns the instances and the errors, for example for an [id] in
use. {!Workero.Workflow.get} finds an instance by id. An instance has
{!Workero.Workflow.Instance.val-status}, [pause], [resume], [terminate], [restart]
and {!Workero.Workflow.Instance.send_event}. The calls raise
{!Workero.Js_error} when the runtime rejects them: a missing instance, an [id]
in use, [resume] of an instance that runs, [send_event] to an instance that is
not running.

{1 Limits}

The runtime enforces its
{{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/workflows/reference/limits/}limits}: 1,024
steps (free) or 10,000 (paid, up to 25,000) per instance, 1 MiB for a step
result and for an event payload, 365 days for a sleep, and an instance id of up
to 100 characters. See {{!page-known_issues}Troubleshooting and support} for
what workero leaves out and for what its tests prove.
